  "faqs": [
    {
      "question": "What is the most common mortgage type?",
      "answer": "Conforming conventional loans are the most common type. Conventional loans make up the majority of mortgages, and conforming loans follow maximum loan amounts set by the government plus other rules set by Fannie Mae or Freddie Mac. On rate type, most borrowers choose a fixed-rate mortgage."
    },
    {
      "question": "What are the five major types of mortgages?",
      "answer": "The CFPB sorts mortgages into three categories: conventional, government and special programs, and the overview table splits them into six rows: conforming, jumbo, FHA, VA, USDA and state or local housing agency programs. Each type can then pair with a loan term and a fixed or adjustable rate."
    },
    {
      "question": "What is a jumbo mortgage loan?",
      "answer": "A jumbo mortgage is a loan above the conforming loan limit, which the Federal Housing Finance Agency publishes each year and which varies by county. Some counties allow conforming jumbo loans above the standard limit. Loans above that county figure are non-conforming jumbo loans."
    },
    {
      "question": "What is a hybrid ARM mortgage?",
      "answer": "A hybrid ARM is an adjustable-rate mortgage that starts with a fixed rate for an introductory period, then adjusts regularly after that. Once adjustments begin, the rate follows an index plus a set margin, subject to any caps, so the monthly payment can rise or fall. The CFPB warns against assuming you can sell or refinance before the rate changes."
    },
    {
      "question": "What are the risks of a mortgage?",
      "answer": "Risks the CFPB names include rising ARM payments and risky loan features. An ARM payment can go up a lot after the fixed period, even double. Prepayment penalties, balloon payments, negative amortization and interest-only payments all call for caution, and nonqualified mortgages can be riskier or more expensive. Ask for a Loan Estimate without the feature to compare."
    },
    {
      "question": "What is the best type of home mortgage loan?",
      "answer": "No single type is best for every buyer. The right fit depends on your down payment, your credit, which programs you are eligible for, and how long you plan to stay in the home. Compare Loan Estimates for every type that fits you to see which one costs least overall."
    }
  ],
